// case: at-out-of-range
// what: an index past the end of a string is not a character. `orbit_string_at`
//      (`runtime/collections.c:369-373`) answered 0 for `index >= len`, which is
//      indistinguishable from the character NUL, and the front end had both the
//      receiver and the index and raised nothing. Measured: `s.at(99)` over
//      "hello" printed 0. The receiver here is a real string, so this isolates
//      the missing bounds check from n31's receiver-type bug: with the receiver
//      correct, an in-range index is right and an out-of-range one is silently
//      zero.
//
//      Nothing in std/ depends on this being 0 -- `getOr` guards with
//      `i >= l.len()` before it calls `get` -- so it can be made an error
//      without a coordinated std change. FINDINGS F-0016.
//
//      FIXED. The front end now proves this one while compiling, because it has
//      both halves: `s` is an immutable binding to a literal with no escapes in
//      it, and 99 is a literal. The runtime half covers what the front end
//      cannot see (a computed index, a list built by push) and is
//      tests/suite/list_index_out_of_range.orb, which exits 2.
//
//      The position on the card comes from a new field on CallNode and
//      IndexAccessNode: those were the only two nodes in the AST with no
//      position, and a check that cannot say where it is has to fall back to
//      the one-line form.
// expect-error: String index 99 is out of range for "s", which holds 5 characters
fn main() -> int {
    val s = "hello"
    print(s.at(99))
    return 0
}
